module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.math.checked_mul

// What a FUSED PAIR does: one function per superinstruction, each of them the two arms the emitter
// wrote run back to back with the value between them left in a register instead of on the stack.
//
// **WHAT IS BOUGHT IS THE INSTRUCTION FETCH, AND THE FETCH IS NOT CHEAP HERE.** `Buf.at<Ins>` is an
// out-of-line call that retains the code buffer, bounds-checks it twice and copies 56 bytes, and
// `bench/results/2026-09-21-sampled-profile.md`'s sampled profile puts it at **12.4% of all sampled
// time** -- one per instruction
// executed. Two instructions run as one pay it once. The push and the pop of the intermediate value
// go with it, which on `AddStoreSlot` and `LessJumpIfFalse` is a `Buf.push<Value>` and a truncate
// that existed only to move a number four bytes.
//
// **WHICH PAIRS WAS MEASURED AND NOT GUESSED.** `profile.sysl` counts adjacent pairs and triples
// under `--features profile`, and every sequence here was the heaviest broad one when it was fused:
// the first four pairs (`LoadSlot`+`PushInt`, `LoadSlot`+`LoadSlot`, `Add`+`StoreSlot`,
// `Less`+`JumpIfFalse`), then `Sub`+`StoreSlot` and the two five-slot runs a counted loop's head and
// tail make of those pairs. Re-run that table before proposing another; an arm costs a slot in the
// hottest `match` in the project, and `code.sysl` carries the numbers.
//
// **EVERY ONE KEEPS THE INT-INT FAST PATH INLINE AND HANDS EVERYTHING ELSE TO `run_arith.sysl`.**
// There is no second copy of an overflow promotion, a string join, an operator method or a
// diagnostic anywhere in this file: a pair of machine integers is answered here and every other pair
// goes to the very function the unfused instruction called. That is what makes a fused pair
// answer-for-answer identical to the two it replaces, which `tests_fused.sysl` asserts rather than
// assumes.
//
// **A FUSED BODY IS A CALL ON PURPOSE, AND WRITING THE FOUR OUT INSIDE THE DISPATCH WAS MEASURED AND
// UNDONE.** They were written out in `run_frames.sysl`'s own arms for one release, which measured
// -1.96% of the geometric mean against a compiler whose `Buf.push` was still an out-of-line call --
// and +1.19% against sysl 0.0.125, which inlines that push into the very same function. The two
// cannot both have the registers: `run_frames` already used every callee-saved register the ABI has
// and already opened a 1,504-byte frame, so the allocator answers more live values by spilling three
// of the loop's own nine parameters at entry rather than by growing the frame -- `u`, which every
// instruction fetch reads, among them, reloaded forty-two times across the body.
// `bench/results/2026-09-22-unfuse.md` has the three-way measurement and
// `bench/results/2026-09-22-sampled-profile-4.md` the disassembly. **So a fast path whose caller is
// register-saturated is cheaper as a call, and the cost of the `bl` is the smaller of the two.**
//
// **NOTHING HERE SAYS `@noinline` AND NOTHING NEEDS TO.** `private` in sysl is private to the FILE,
// so a body called from `run_frames.sysl` and written in this one cannot be `private` -- and a
// module-visible name lowers to an LLVM `define` with EXTERNAL linkage, whose body must be kept
// whatever the inliner decides, so the single-caller bonus that would bring one under the `-O2`
// threshold never applies. `nm` finds all four symbols in the binary and `otool` exactly one `bl` to
// each. **The file split is what keeps them out of line**, which is the same finding read backwards:
// moving one into the caller's file is what inlines it, and that is the change this page undid.
//
// **THE SECOND INSTRUCTION IS STILL IN THE CODE BUFFER AND MUST STAY THERE.** `fuse` in `emit.sysl`
// overwrites the first slot of a pair and leaves the second exactly as the emitter wrote it, so a
// jump that lands on it runs the unfused instruction and gets the unfused answer -- which is why no
// fusion here needs anything proved about jump targets. The loop steps over that slot with an extra
// `pc += 1`; forget it and the tail runs twice.
//
// Nothing here is `private`: every name is reached from `run_frames.sysl`, which is the only caller
// of any of them.

// `LoadSlot(i)` then `LoadSlot(j)`.
//
// **Read both cells BEFORE pushing either**, because a push may grow the stack and replace its
// storage -- and `vm.stack.at` would then be reading the old block. The unfused pair could not have
// this problem, each of its instructions doing one thing.
fused_load_slot2(vm: *Machine, base: usize, i: usize, j: usize)
    val a = vm.stack.at(base + i)
    val b = vm.stack.at(base + j)

    vm.stack.push(a)
    vm.stack.push(b)

// `LoadSlot(i)` then `PushInt(v)`.
fused_load_slot_int(vm: *Machine, base: usize, i: usize, v: long)
    val a = vm.stack.at(base + i)

    vm.stack.push(a)
    vm.stack.push(Int(v))

// `Add` then `StoreSlot(i)`.
//
// **A whole-number sum goes straight into the cell and is never pushed**, which is the whole of what
// this pair is worth beyond the fetch: the unfused form pushed the answer, fetched a `StoreSlot`,
// popped it back off and asked `keepable` about it. **An `Int` can never be `undefined`**, so the
// absence rule has nothing to say about the fast path and the check is not skipped so much as
// answered.
//
// **IT HANDS THE VALUE BACK RATHER THAN STORING IT WHERE THE SUM IS NOT WHOLE, AND THAT IS ABOUT A
// SPAN.** The absence refusal belongs to the `StoreSlot` and has to point where the unfused one
// pointed -- a different character from the `Add` this instruction carries -- and reading that span
// means fetching the instruction the fusion stepped over, which is the very cost the fusion exists
// to remove. So the fast path, where the answer is an `Int` and can need no such sentence, stores
// and answers `None`; everything else answers the value and lets the loop pay for the span it now
// genuinely needs. `stored_in_slot` below is the other half.
fused_add_store_slot(vm: *Machine, base: usize, i: usize, at: Span) -> Result[Option[Value], Signal]
    val b = pop_on(vm)
    val a = pop_on(vm)

    a match
        Int(x) -> b match
            Int(y) ->
                val sum = x + y

                if (x ^ sum) & (y ^ sum) >= 0
                    vm.stack.set(base + i, Int(sum))
                    return Ok(None)
            Real(y) ->
                vm.stack.set(base + i, Real(real(x) + y))
                return Ok(None)
            _ -> ()

        // **A real is never an absence**, so a sum with one in it goes into the cell with no
        // `keepable` question, exactly as a whole number does. `added`'s own promotion, written here,
        // after the integer arm. (Moved out of line behind `@noinline`, `loops` -- two whole numbers
        // through this instruction -- read +9.1% rather than +3.5%: the swing is the PGO layout of the
        // whole loop, not these arms. `bench/results/2026-09-24-reals-inline.md` has both.)
        Real(x) -> b match
            Real(y) ->
                vm.stack.set(base + i, Real(x + y))
                return Ok(None)
            Int(y) ->
                vm.stack.set(base + i, Real(x + real(y)))
                return Ok(None)
            _ -> ()
        _ -> ()

    Ok(Some(added(a, b, at)?))
end fused_add_store_slot

// What an unfused `StoreSlot` does with a value that is already in hand, which is the tail
// `fused_add_store_slot` hands back.
stored_in_slot(vm: *Machine, base: usize, i: usize, v: Value, at: Span) -> Result[unit, Signal]
    keepable(v, at, "written to a local")?
    vm.stack.set(base + i, v)
    Ok(())

// `Less` then `JumpIfFalse(to)`, answering whether the jump is taken.
//
// **`pc` stays the loop's**, which is what lets this be a function at all: every other thing the
// pair does is the operand stack's, and the one thing that is not is handed back as a boolean.
//
// **The machine is a parameter for `pop_on`'s reason** -- the two operands come off the stack the
// loop is already holding, so asking `current()` for it again is a thread-local lookup on the arm
// that carries every loop condition in the suite.
fused_less_jump_if_false(vm: *Machine, at: Span) -> Result[bool, Signal]
    val b = pop_on(vm)
    val a = pop_on(vm)

    a match
        Int(x) -> b match
            Int(y) -> return Ok(!(x < y))
            _ -> ()
        _ -> ()

    Ok(!is_less(a, b, at)?.truthy())
end fused_less_jump_if_false

// `Sub` then `StoreSlot(i)`, which is `fused_add_store_slot` with the other operator and answers the
// same way: `None` where the difference went into the cell, and the value where the store's own
// span is owed.
fused_sub_store_slot(vm: *Machine, base: usize, i: usize, at: Span) -> Result[Option[Value], Signal]
    val b = pop_on(vm)
    val a = pop_on(vm)

    a match
        Int(x) -> b match
            Int(y) ->
                val diff = x - y

                // A difference overflows exactly where the operands' signs differ and the answer's
                // sign is not the minuend's.
                if (x ^ y) & (x ^ diff) >= 0
                    vm.stack.set(base + i, Int(diff))
                    return Ok(None)
            Real(y) ->
                vm.stack.set(base + i, Real(real(x) - y))
                return Ok(None)
            _ -> ()
        Real(x) -> b match
            Real(y) ->
                vm.stack.set(base + i, Real(x - y))
                return Ok(None)
            Int(y) ->
                vm.stack.set(base + i, Real(x - real(y)))
                return Ok(None)
            _ -> ()
        _ -> ()

    Ok(Some(subtracted(a, b, at)?))
end fused_sub_store_slot

// -- THE FUSED RUNS: a loop's head and its tail -----------------------------------------------------
//
// **Each fast path answers without reading a slot it stepped over**, which is the point: the exit
// target of a loop head and the spans of every instruction after the first are in the code buffer,
// and the arm in `run_frames.sysl` reads one only on the path that owes it -- the loop ending, or a
// value that is not a whole number and may have a sentence coming.

// `Tick`, then whether `LoadSlot(i) < v`: `Some` with the answer where the slot holds a whole
// number, and `None` where it does not and the ordinary comparison has to be asked.
//
// **The safe point is the Tick arm's own two lines**, the collection and the full-heap sentence at
// the Tick's span, which is the first slot's and so the one this instruction carries.
fused_tick_slot_int_less(here: *Vm, vm: *Machine, base: usize, i: usize, v: long, at: Span) -> Result[Option[bool], Signal]
    maybe_collect_on(here)

    if here.heap_exhausted then return Err(failure(at, "this program has run out of memory"))

    vm.stack.at(base + i) match
        Int(x) -> Ok(Some(x < v))
        _ -> Ok(None)
end fused_tick_slot_int_less

// `LoadSlot(i) < v` for a slot that is not a whole number, with the `Less`'s own span.
fused_slot_less(vm: *Machine, base: usize, i: usize, v: long, at: Span) -> Result[bool, Signal]
    Ok(is_less(vm.stack.at(base + i), Int(v), at)?.truthy())

// Where a loop head's `JumpIfFalse` goes, read out of the slot the run stepped over. `fuse` writes a
// head only in front of one, so any other instruction here is a defect in `fuse`.
fused_exit(op: Op) -> usize = op match
    JumpIfFalse(to) -> to
    _ -> panic("a fused loop head is not followed by its JumpIfFalse")

// `i = i + v` on a slot that holds a whole number and does not overflow, written straight into the
// cell; `false` where the ordinary addition has to be asked.
fused_slot_int_add(vm: *Machine, base: usize, i: usize, v: long) -> bool
    vm.stack.at(base + i) match
        Int(x) ->
            val sum = x + v

            if (x ^ sum) & (v ^ sum) < 0 then return false

            vm.stack.set(base + i, Int(sum))
            true
        _ -> false
end fused_slot_int_add

// `i = i + v` the long way, with the `Add`'s span for the arithmetic and the `StoreSlot`'s for the
// absence rule -- the two sentences the unfused run would have said.
fused_slot_int_add_slow(vm: *Machine, base: usize, i: usize, v: long, add_at: Span, store_at: Span) -> Result[unit, Signal]
    val sum = added(vm.stack.at(base + i), Int(v), add_at)?

    stored_in_slot(vm, base, i, sum, store_at)

// -- A PRODUCT SUMMED, A LOCAL'S FIELD, A REMAINDER BY A LITERAL, A MODULE VARIABLE AND A LITERAL ----

// `Mul` then `Add`, `c + a * b` with all three whole numbers and neither step overflowing, or with a
// real among them: `true` where the answer is standing, and `false` with the stack untouched where
// the ordinary two are owed.
//
// **The real arms are `multiplied` then `added`, each promoting exactly as it does** -- the product
// is a real as soon as either factor is, and a whole-number product that fits meets a real `c` as
// `real(product)`. Two rounded steps, never a fused multiply-add, which would round once and answer
// a different last bit than the unfused pair.
fused_mul_add(vm: *Machine) -> bool
    val b = peek_on(vm, 0)
    val a = peek_on(vm, 1)
    val c = peek_on(vm, 2)

    a match
        Int(x) -> b match
            Int(y) -> checked_mul(x, y) match
                Some(product) -> c match
                    Int(z) ->
                        val sum = z + product

                        if (z ^ sum) & (product ^ sum) < 0 then return false

                        mul_add_answer(vm, Int(sum))
                    Real(z) -> mul_add_answer(vm, Real(z + real(product)))
                    _ -> false
                None -> false
            Real(y) -> real_product_summed(vm, c, real(x) * y)
            _ -> false
        Real(x) -> b match
            Real(y) -> real_product_summed(vm, c, x * y)
            Int(y) -> real_product_summed(vm, c, x * real(y))
            _ -> false
        _ -> false
end fused_mul_add

// `c + product` for a real product, answered where `c` is a number and handed back otherwise.
@inline
private real_product_summed(vm: *Machine, c: Value, product: real) -> bool = c match
    Real(z) -> mul_add_answer(vm, Real(z + product))
    Int(z) -> mul_add_answer(vm, Real(real(z) + product))
    _ -> false

// The three operands off, the answer standing where `c` stood.
@inline
private mul_add_answer(vm: *Machine, v: Value) -> bool
    vm.stack.truncate(vm.stack.len() - 2)
    vm.stack.set(vm.stack.len() - 1, v)
    true

// The two the long way, each with its own span: the `Mul`'s for the product and the `Add`'s, read out
// of the slot stepped over, for the sum.
fused_mul_add_slow(vm: *Machine, mul_at: Span, add_at: Span) -> Result[unit, Signal]
    val b = pop_on(vm)
    val a = pop_on(vm)
    val product = multiplied(a, b, mul_at)?
    val c = pop_on(vm)

    vm.stack.push(added(c, product, add_at)?)
    Ok(())

// `LoadSlot(i)` then `GetField(k, c)`: the local pushed, and its field read over it -- the subject
// stands on the stack across the read, which is the `GetField` arm's own rule and its reason.
fused_load_slot_get_field(vm: *Machine, u: *Unit, base: usize, i: usize, c: usize, k: usize, at: Span) -> Result[unit, Signal]
    vm.stack.push(vm.stack.at(base + i))
    vm.stack.set(vm.stack.len() - 1, read_field_site(peek_on(vm, 0), u, k, c, at)?)
    Ok(())

// `LoadSlot(i)` then `GetIndex`, the container standing and the index in the cell at `cell`. **An
// array at a whole number inside it is read where it stands** -- the index never pushed, the
// container's place on the stack taken by the element -- and everything else is the `GetIndex` arm
// itself with the local pushed first, so every other reading and every sentence is `read_at`'s.
fused_load_slot_index(vm: *Machine, cell: usize, at: Span) -> Result[unit, Signal]
    val k = vm.stack.at(cell)

    if !indexed_in_place(vm, vm.stack.len() - 1, k)
        vm.stack.push(k)
        compose_get_index(vm, at)?

    Ok(())

// `LoadSlot(i)`, `PushInt(v)`, `Rem`: the remainder pushed, with the `Rem`'s span for its faults.
fused_slot_int_rem(vm: *Machine, base: usize, i: usize, v: long, at: Span) -> Result[unit, Signal]
    vm.stack.push(remainder_of(vm.stack.at(base + i), Int(v), at)?)
    Ok(())

// `LoadCell(d, k)` then `PushInt(v)`: the `LoadCell` arm, and the literal after it.
fused_load_cell_int(u: *Unit, vm: *Machine, here: *Vm, d: usize, k: usize, v: long, at: Span) -> Result[unit, Signal]
    val cell = if d < here.defs.len() then here.defs.at(d) else Undefined

    cell match
        Undefined -> compose_load_cell(u, vm, k, at)?
        found -> vm.stack.push(found)

    vm.stack.push(Int(v))
    Ok(())
end fused_load_cell_int

// -- THE SAME FOR A MODULE-LEVEL `var`: its cell in `Vm.defs` where a local has its slot -------------
//
// **A top-level loop's names cannot be slots** -- an importer reads them by name -- so each read and
// write is a `LoadCell`/`StoreCell`, and `bench/globals.sl`'s loop was thirteen dispatches a turn
// where `arith.sl`'s is seven. These four are the slot runs above over a cell, and each keeps the
// same bargain: a whole number in a FILLED cell is answered here, and everything else -- an empty
// cell, which is a read that has run too early and takes the lookup, a sum that overflows, a value
// that is not an integer -- runs exactly what the unfused instructions ran, with each one's own span
// read out of the slot it stands in.
//
// **Each answers the `pc` the loop goes on from**, so its arm in `run_frames.sysl` is one line: that
// file is at its thousand-line limit, and `pc` is the one piece of the loop's own state these touch.
// `pc` is handed in as the loop holds it, one past the fused instruction -- the first slot stepped
// over.

// `Add` then `StoreCell(d, k)`.
fused_add_store_cell(u: *Unit, here: *Vm, vm: *Machine, d: usize, k: usize, code: Code, pc: usize, at: Span) -> Result[usize, Signal]
    val b = pop_on(vm)
    val a = pop_on(vm)

    a match
        Int(x) -> b match
            Int(y) ->
                val sum = x + y

                if (x ^ sum) & (y ^ sum) >= 0 && def_filled(here, d)
                    here.defs.set(d, Int(sum))
                    return Ok(pc + 1)
            _ -> ()
        _ -> ()

    vm.stack.push(added(a, b, at)?)
    compose_store_cell(u, vm, here, d, k, code.ins[pc].at)?
    Ok(pc + 1)
end fused_add_store_cell

// `Sub` then `StoreCell(d, k)`, the same with the other operator.
fused_sub_store_cell(u: *Unit, here: *Vm, vm: *Machine, d: usize, k: usize, code: Code, pc: usize, at: Span) -> Result[usize, Signal]
    val b = pop_on(vm)
    val a = pop_on(vm)

    a match
        Int(x) -> b match
            Int(y) ->
                val diff = x - y

                if (x ^ y) & (x ^ diff) >= 0 && def_filled(here, d)
                    here.defs.set(d, Int(diff))
                    return Ok(pc + 1)
            _ -> ()
        _ -> ()

    vm.stack.push(subtracted(a, b, at)?)
    compose_store_cell(u, vm, here, d, k, code.ins[pc].at)?
    Ok(pc + 1)
end fused_sub_store_cell

// `Tick; LoadCell(d, k); PushInt(v); Less; JumpIfFalse(to)`. The safe point is the `Tick` arm's own
// two lines at the `Tick`'s span; the spelling, the `Less`'s span and the exit are read out of the
// slots stepped over, and only on the path that owes them.
fused_tick_cell_int_less(u: *Unit, here: *Vm, vm: *Machine, d: usize, v: long, code: Code, pc: usize, at: Span) -> Result[usize, Signal]
    maybe_collect_on(here)

    if here.heap_exhausted then return Err(failure(at, "this program has run out of memory"))

    val cell = if d < here.defs.len() then here.defs.at(d) else Undefined
    val goes = cell match
        Int(x) -> x < v
        _ ->
            val k = code.ins[pc].op match
                LoadCellInt(_, key, _) -> usize(key)
                _ -> panic("a fused loop head over a cell is not standing over its LoadCellInt")

            fused_load_cell_int(u, vm, here, d, k, v, code.ins[pc].at)?
            !fused_less_jump_if_false(vm, code.ins[pc + 2].at)?

    Ok(if goes then pc + 4 else fused_exit(code.ins[pc + 3].op))
end fused_tick_cell_int_less

// `LoadCell(d, k); PushInt(v); Add; StoreCell(d, k); Jump(to)`, answering `to` either way.
fused_cell_int_add_store_jump(u: *Unit, here: *Vm, vm: *Machine, d: usize, v: long, to: usize, code: Code, pc: usize, at: Span) -> Result[usize, Signal]
    val cell = if d < here.defs.len() then here.defs.at(d) else Undefined

    cell match
        Int(x) ->
            val sum = x + v

            if (x ^ sum) & (v ^ sum) >= 0
                here.defs.set(d, Int(sum))
                return Ok(to)
        _ -> ()

    val k = code.ins[pc + 2].op match
        StoreCell(_, key) -> usize(key)
        _ -> panic("a fused loop tail over a cell is not standing over its StoreCell")

    cell match
        Undefined -> compose_load_cell(u, vm, k, at)?
        found -> vm.stack.push(found)

    val a = pop_on(vm)

    vm.stack.push(added(a, Int(v), code.ins[pc + 1].at)?)
    compose_store_cell(u, vm, here, d, k, code.ins[pc + 2].at)?
    Ok(to)
end fused_cell_int_add_store_jump
